<div class="meta">Drill 0021 · pairs with lesson 0021</div>
<h1>Drill: The World Bus and Injected Time</h1>

<div class="q"><h3>1. How does a test prove an NPC has no private clock?</h3>
<details><summary>Answer</summary><div class="a"><p>Call the same NPC's
<code>update</code> with different <code>timeOfDayHours</code> values and
watch its destination change. Only the parameter varied — if the NPC
cached time anywhere, the second call couldn't behave differently.</p>
</div></details></div>

<div class="q"><h3>2. Why do the sky, fog, and light all sample the SAME
hour, and what breaks if fog keeps a daylight constant?</h3>
<details><summary>Answer</summary><div class="a"><p>Fog's whole trick is
melting geometry into the sky (lesson 0019). A daylight fog color under a
midnight sky puts a bright blue haze on a dark horizon — distant buildings
would glow. All three derive from one clock so they can never disagree.</p>
</div></details></div>

<div class="q"><h3>3. What made the bus "generic enough" without
over-building?</h3>
<details><summary>Answer</summary><div class="a"><p>String keys with a
number-and/or-text fact per key — enough for the clock and for gossip's
structured section to move in later without reshaping the store. What was
deliberately NOT built: subscriptions, persistence, replication — none had
a consumer yet.</p></div></details></div>

<div class="q"><h3>4. Why does --hour exist for smoke runs?</h3>
<details><summary>Answer</summary><div class="a"><p>Day/night made every
screenshot depend on when the frames ran. Pinning the clock restores
deterministic verification — the same reason smoke runs already pin the
camera and skip mouse look.</p></div></details></div>
